Jim Miller <yohocoma@yahoo.com> wrote:
>*Of course* testing and bug-freeness are parts of a good development
>model.  And so is communication with the user base, and so is a
>release schedule that meets the needs of users.  The needs of users,
>with their bug identification and their ideas and their overall
>enthusiasm for growth and new challenges in the game, far outstrips
>"the devteam"'s "it'll be done when it's done" attitude.

It's a *game*, Mr Miller.  Get a grip.  Game users don't have needs,
they have desires.  Now, if the software package in question was, say,
GCC or Mozilla or the Linux kernel, you might have a point.

And, the DevTeam do communicate with the user base.  At least one member
of the DevTeam has been known to post to RGRN.  When I reported a bug in
a then-current version of Nethack (3.1.3), I received e-mail from a member
of the DevTeam saying what amounted to "Ah, yes.  That is quite a nasty
bug.  As it happens, we'd already come up with a fix, but thank you for
your report!"

I also note that at least one of the bugs on the "Spoiler bugs" list for
3.4.3 is a bug I found (and reported indirectly), so they *do* listen to
the users.
